    episode: "Series cutaway",
    stylePrompt: "Deeply sentimental, tear-stained slide-steel guitar ballad in the atmospheric style of Daniel Lanois (Sling Blade OST, \"The Maker\", Emmylou Harris Wrecking Ball era). 4 - 5 minutes, instrumental, no vocals. This is the most openly emotional and melodic piece in the series — a wordless farewell that aches. The heavily processed pedal steel carries a real, memorable, singing melody: long-lined, vocal, human, phrased like someone trying not to cry. Signature Lanois processing throughout — cavernous Lexicon reverb tails, shimmering octave-shifted delays doubling the melody a beat behind like a distant echo of memory, soft granular smearing on the sustains, warm analog tape saturation and gentle controlled feedback. Still no blues or country clichés: the harmony is warm but ambiguous, built on suspended and major-seventh chords with a quietly descending bassline that pulls at the heart, never landing on a tidy cadence. Expressive volume-pedal swells give every note a violin-soft attack that blooms and sustains; graceful glissandos slide up into each melody note from below, catching in the throat. Delicate high harmonics chime like held-back tears. Phrasing is extremely slow and rubato, breathing, with long silences between phrases where the reverb weeps on alone. Dynamics rise and fall like waves of grief and acceptance, building to one aching high sustained note near the end that finally releases and dissolves into stillness. Mood: profound tenderness, loss, love, and a fragile peace at the end — a long goodbye at a bedside or a window, warm golden light fading, the steel singing the words no one could say out loud.",
